<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Noukouma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina-jihad">Massina Jihad</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Noukouma was fought in 1818 in the inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> delta of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. The Fulbe preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a> and his followers defeated the forces of the Fulbe ardo chiefs and their allies from the Bamana Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Ségou</a>. The victory opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> that created the Massina Empire, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdullahi">Hamdullahi</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Fulbe of the Middle Niger had for centuries been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of larger states, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> to the Bambara Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Ségou</a>. By the early 1800s many of these states had weakened, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a>, a preacher from a minor scholarly family of a lesser clan, inspired by the risings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> in Hausaland, began a religious revival that set him against the established elites. In 1816 or 1817 Arɗo Guidado, son of the arɗo Arɗo Amadu, stole a blanket from one of Seku Amadu's followers at Simay, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a>, and when he refused to return it and insulted the preacher, the follower killed him.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a> withdrew to Noukouma, gathered supporters and, through a delegation, pledged allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, which authorised him to wage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> and sent him a flag. Arɗo Amadu won the backing of Da Diarra, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Ségou</a>, and of other arɗos, and by one account mobilised over 200,000 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>By the time of the battle on 21 March 1818, about 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marka">Marka</a>, Bozo and others had gathered around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a>, armed with bows, arrows and spears, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bamana</a> army under General Jamogo Séri, given as 100,000 strong, had rifles. Seku Amadu likened the day to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-badr">Battle of Badr</a>.</p><p>The fighting began when a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> of Yatenga ran up and shot an arrow into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bamana</a> war drum and was gunned down. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a> then formed his men into three fronts of 313, the number of Badr, and advanced straight on Séri's camp. The Bamana took this for a retreat by their general, and in the confusion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> cattle, frightened by the gunfire, stampeded into them. Séri was killed and his army fell back to a hastily fortified camp at Yeri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a> took the victory as a divine miracle, and new recruits and deserters from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bamana</a> army soon raised his force to over 40,000. His state took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> in 1819 and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdullahi">Hamdullahi</a> in 1820. Gelaajo, ruler of a kingdom centred on Goundaka, submitted after the battle, but when a cousin of Seku Amadu was made governor in his place he revolted in 1824. No longer needing outside support, and after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, Seku Amadu broke his allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Noukouma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1818 Seku Amadu, a Fulbe preacher in the Massina region of the inner Niger delta, led a revolt against the Fulbe ardo chiefs and their Bambara overlords of the Bamana Empire, winning a battle at <b>Noukouma</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/massina-empire">Massina Empire</a></li></ul></section>
